WEBVTT

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:13.330
Hello, I am Shaurya Sharma. I worked on Verilog implementation,
test benches,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.970
simulation and performance analysis and synthesis.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.550
Hello, I'm Lakshay Saharan.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:23.750
I worked on the project report, presentation slides, and the
demonstration

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:28.610
video. Our project studies how a cache between the CPU and the
main memory

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.770
handles, requests, and the benefits from the locality.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.370
The cache stores 1 kilobyte of data using 16 byte blocks.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:42.510
Our report explains the design, verification method, measured
results,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:46.710
and the limitations. Shaurya will now demonstrate the
implementations.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:54.720
The cache has 64 lines with 4 32 bits words per line, each 32
bit address

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.040
contains a 22 bit tag, a 6 bit index and a 4 bit offset.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.140
The index selects a line, a hit occurs when its valid bit is set
and store

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:06.840
tag matches the request tag.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:12.740
I divided the implementation into an address decoder, tag
comparator,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.920
metadata storage, data ram and controller.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.580
Requests and responses use valid ready handshakes, the
controller handles

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.480
one CPU request at a time.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.500
The controller uses seven states.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.580
IDLE accepts the request and lookup checks for a hit.

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.880
A read hit returns the cached word.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:42.360
A read miss fetches all four words from memory, then marks the
completed

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:47.780
line valid. The write policy is write through with no write
allocate.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.720
A write hit updates both cache and memory.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.420
A write miss updates memory without loading a new cache block.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.500
The responses stay stable until the CPU accepts it.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.140
Reset clears the valid bits and cancels the outstanding work
when the

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.520
memory interface resets along the cache.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.630
These are actual simulation results.

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.750
Independent Verilog and Python reference models check return
data and

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.670
hit or miss decisions.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:24.750
Tests also cover writes, conflicting addresses, responses,
stalls and

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:29.610
reset. In this direct trace, address 0 initially misses.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:35.350
Reads at address 4, 8 and 12 then hit because the first request
loads

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:41.310
the entire block. Address 1024 maps to the same cache line and
cause a

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:46.450
conflict miss. The waveform show a refill and the response
handshakes.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:54.110
Across both cache and configurations, the baseline FPGA netlist
18,504

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:59.550
trace transactions passed plus separate reset tests.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.800
Sequential reads achieved a 75% hit rate.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:11.780
Repeated reads achieved 96.88% while alternating conflicting
addresses

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:14.460
produce 0% hits.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:21.500
Repeated reads were about 1.83 times faster than the no-cache
baseline

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.500
in the mean response latency.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:30.520
Sequentially and conflict access were slower because each cache
miss fetches

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:36.860
four words serially. Reference synthesis and routing for a
Lattice iCE40

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:44.980
HX8K uses 610 logic cells and 4 block RAMs.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:53.520
The estimated internal maximum frequency was 75.35 MHz meeting
the 25

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.740
MHz target. These are tool estimates.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.020
We did not test a physical FPGA board.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:06.730
The IEEE style report includes the abstract, literature review,
proposed

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:10.310
solution, individual contributions and references.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:16.250
It also presents the architecture, state machine, waveform and
performance

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:21.270
comparison. The slide summarizes the results for the
demonstration.

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:27.110
The repository contains our Verilog modules, test benches,
access traces,

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:29.970
simulation evidence, report and presentation.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:37.090
Our results show that locality improves repeated access
performance while

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:41.090
conflict misses and serial refill can increase latency.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:46.470
Possible improvements include burst refill and write buffer.
Thank you.
